{
  "title": "Co-designing an Integrated Health and Social Care Hub With and for Families Experiencing Adversity",
  "authors": "Teresa Hall; Sarah Loveday; Sandie Pullen; Hayley Loftus; Leanne Constable; Kate Paton; Harriet Hiscock",
  "decision": "Include",
  "evidence": {
    "HCD_fullcycle": [
      // A1: User Involvement
      "Stage 1 established governance mechanisms and partnerships with a local project advisory group that included representation from health, community and education service providers, local government, and a community representative with lived experience. Lived experience was prioritised as a type of expertise central to the project in all communications and research activities.",
      "Families experiencing adversity were actively engaged throughout the four-stage process, with recruitment materials specifying engagement with caregivers without requiring disclosure of adversity, focusing on learning from families’ experiences of services.",
      "Stage 4 involved co-design workshops and consultations with intersectoral practitioners and families, using a human-centred design process and integrating lived and professional experience in the Hub’s co-design."
    ],
    "TASKS_elements": [
      "Resourcing (i.e., time, human resources) was reported as a challenge to the co-design process, indicating designer-facing strain in allocating resources for participation and prototyping.",
      "A substantial time investment was required to plan and execute the workshops and consultations, highlighting workload and scheduling pressures on designers and participants.",
      "Practitioners described the process as very intensive, with concerns about turnover times for prototyping and the need to juggle project commitments with routine work.",
      "Community participants noted the long overall commitment required to attend all sessions, with some prefers to drop in and out rather than commit to every session.",
      "COVID-19 impacted stakeholder engagement, as Wyndham was a hot spot for transmission and families faced additional stress, affecting participation and engagement levels."
    ]
  },
  "reasoning": [
    "Condition A (Full HCD Cycle) is satisfied: The paper explicitly describes user involvement across multiple stages (Stage 1 governance with lived experience; Stage 2 formative research engaging families; Stage 3 personas and client journeys; Stage 4 co-design workshops with iterative testing and prototyping). It also indicates iterative elements (recurrent design activities, testing, and refinement) and covers all four lifecycle phases (Understanding users, Ideation, Prototyping, Evaluation).",
    "Condition B (TASKS Elements) is satisfied: The core text reports designer- and facilitator-facing challenges, including resource constraints, time burdens, intense engagement demands, and the impact of COVID-19 on participation, indicating designer experience of difficulty and strain during the design process."
  ]
}

{
  "title": "User-Centred Design and Usability Evaluation of a Heart Rate Variability Biofeedback Game",
  "authors": "Thomas Wollmann, Farhad Abtahi, Aboozar Eghdam, Fernando Seoane, Kaj Lindecrantz, Martin Haag, Sabine Koch",
  "decision": "Include",
  "evidence": {
    "HCD_fullcycle": [
      "The paper states that 'design, development, and evaluation were conducted using a user-centred approach, including context analysis, the design of game principle mock-ups, the selection of game principles through a survey, the design of the game mechanics and GUI mock-up, and icon testing, and the performance of a summative study through user questionnaires and interviews.'",
      "Formative evaluations through 'icon tests, unstructured interviews and theoretic models' were conducted, and there were two iterations of the icon testing method, indicating iterative refinement.",
      "Context analysis and game principle selection are described, including generating five game concepts and selecting the preferred concept based on a survey of 20 participants; the process includes understanding the target users and deriving design directions from user input."
    ],
    "TASKS_elements": [
      "One of the authors (TW) evaluated the test procedure on himself, followed by five additional test sessions, indicating designer involvement in identifying and addressing procedural challenges.",
      "The study protocol and materials were 'iteratively improved' during a pilot test, showing iterative refinement driven by developer/experimenter experience.",
      "The SCCs (successful completion criteria) were balanced by 'estimating the difficulty of the tasks,' reflecting designer awareness of task difficulty and workload during study design.",
      "A moderator guide was created based on the experience gained from these test sessions to ensure a repeatable testing procedure, illustrating designer-driven handling of methodological constraints.",
      "During the study, the chosen hardware (HxM BT) 'is not able to measure respiration,' which constrained immediate biofeedback and required design compromises, illustrating a designer-facing limitation in resources."
    ]
  {
    "reasoning": [
      "Reason 1: The study provides clear evidence of a full human-centred design cycle, with user involvement in context analysis, ideation (five concepts via survey), prototyping (GUI/game mechanics), and evaluation (summative usability study with questionnaires and interviews)."
      ,
      "Reason 2: There is explicit documentation of designer-facing challenges and iterations (pilot testing by a designer, iterative protocol/material improvements, task-difficulty balancing, and hardware limitations) indicating designer strain/limitations during the design process."
    ]
  } 
}
